  <p>Your profile allows you to have your own website on Gumroad. You can use it to introduce yourself, <a href="170-audience">grow your following</a> by collecting email addresses, and showcase your products on a fully custom page.</p>
  <p>Your profile is located at [username].gumroad.com where [username] is replaced with your unique username. You can also get there by clicking on your name and then the Profile button on the Gumroad navigation.</p>
  <h3 id="setup">Set up your profile</h3>
  <p>Go to the <a href="https://gumroad.com/settings/profile" target="_blank">profile settings</a> to set your username, profile picture, and a short bio (we’ve found that a sentence or two works best rather than overly-long descriptions of your entire career path). Your name and bio are available to your custom page, so keep them current.</p>
  <h3 id="custom-landing-page">Build your profile page with your agent</h3>
  <p>Your profile page is fully custom HTML, with no fixed layouts, blocks, or sections to work around. It is built to be updated by an AI agent, which writes that HTML for you — there is no in-app editor for it.</p>
  <p>You can use your own AI agent (Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, or whatever you already use), or the Agent tab built into Gumroad. Either way, describe the page you want and the agent builds it, previews it, and publishes it to your profile.</p>
  <p>Click Pages in the Gumroad navigation, open your Home page, and copy the ready-made prompt there into your agent. The prompt asks the agent to design a unique, on-brand, fully responsive page (with light and dark mode), preview it with <code>gumroad pages preview</code>, and publish it with <code>gumroad pages push profile</code>. You can run those same CLI commands by hand if you prefer.</p>
  <p>The published page replaces your entire profile at [username].gumroad.com (and on your custom domain if you have one connected), so have it link visitors to your product pages rather than embedding checkout elements. Custom pages are visible to search engines, with your page's description and products included in structured data automatically.</p>
  <p>If something isn't working, or you want a change you'd rather not make with an agent, email <a href="mailto:support@gumroad.com">support@gumroad.com</a> and we'll take care of it for you. That note appears in every page editor that hands the work to an agent, including the editors for your other pages and for your <a href="353-custom-product-landing-pages" target="_self">product landing pages</a>.</p>
  <h3 id="email-signup-form">Collect email followers on a custom page</h3>
  <p>To collect email addresses for your newsletter from a custom page, ask your agent to include a form marked <code>data-gumroad-follow</code> with an email input and an element marked <code>data-gumroad-follow-message</code> for the confirmation text. Gumroad wires the form to your <a href="170-audience">email audience</a> automatically when the page is served — no extra setup.</p>
  <h3 id="live-prices-Vz7mQ">Show live prices on a custom page</h3>
  <p>Don't type prices into your page as text. A typed price goes stale the moment you edit the product, and it shows every visitor the same number even when their checkout is in another currency. Ask your agent to mark the price instead, and Gumroad fills it in each time the page is served.</p>
  <p>Mark the element with the product's permalink and the field you want: <code>data-gumroad-product="[permalink]"</code> plus <code>data-gumroad-field="price"</code> for the price, <code>"currency"</code> for the three-letter code it is in, or <code>"original-price"</code> for the pre-discount amount. The permalink is the last part of the product's URL. Whatever you write inside the element shows if the permalink doesn't match one of your products, so your set price is a good fallback. Leave the original-price element empty, since it stays empty when the product isn't on sale.</p>
  <p>The price shown is the one a first-time buyer pays: your default discount code is applied, a membership is quoted at its usual billing period, and the amount is converted to the visitor's own currency wherever Gumroad can localize your checkout. If a page builds its cards in JavaScript rather than marking up each price, the same values are also served as a JSON block with the id <code>gumroad-prices</code>, so your agent can read them from there. Both cover the same products as your catalogue data, your newest 100, so on a bigger catalogue keep marked prices to products that appear there. The <a href="https://gumroad.com/api#custom-html" target="_blank">developer documentation</a> lists every field.</p>
  <p>Your <a href="353-custom-product-landing-pages" target="_self">product landing pages</a> use the same <code>data-gumroad-field="price"</code> marker, and there it means the same thing: the price a first-time buyer pays, matching the product page it replaces.</p>
  <h3 id="reset-custom-page">Reset or update your custom page</h3>
  <p>To change the page, ask your agent for the update — it edits and republishes the live page. To start over, open Pages → Home and click Remove custom page; this restores the default template so you can publish a fresh one.</p>
  <h3 id="Standalone-pages-Kw3xR">Add standalone pages to your store</h3>
  <p>Beyond your profile, you can publish standalone pages on your store, like an about page, an FAQ, or licensing terms. Each page gets its own URL at [username].gumroad.com/[page-slug], and on your custom domain if you have one connected.</p>
  <p>To manage your pages, click Pages in the Gumroad navigation. The list shows your profile pinned at the top as your home page (it serves at your store's root and can't be deleted), with your other pages below it.</p>
  <p>Standalone pages can be built the same two ways: ask your agent (each page's editor has a copy-ready prompt, and the agent publishes with <code>gumroad pages push [page-slug]</code>), or click New page and write the content in the rich text editor yourself. Pages built by an agent as fully custom HTML show a preview instead of the rich text editor, so the agent's design can't be accidentally overwritten.</p>
  <p>Live prices and your catalogue data are served with your profile page only, so a standalone page can't mark up a price. Link to the product instead and let its own page show the price.</p>
  <p>To delete a page, click the trash icon next to it in the list. Deleted pages are no longer reachable by visitors, and this can't be undone.</p>
  <p><i>Note:</i> Team members with the marketing or admin role can create and edit pages; support and accountant roles can view them.</p>
  <h3 id="legacy-sections">Legacy pages and sections</h3>
  <p>Profiles used to be laid out with pages of stacked sections (products, posts, subscribe, and text blocks). Sections are being phased out in favor of custom pages: they were confusing to arrange and not customizable enough. If your profile still uses them, the section editor remains available from your profile settings until you publish a custom page. Once your agent-built page is live, it replaces the sections layout entirely.</p>
  <p>If you have no custom page, no products, and no sections, your profile shows an email signup form.</p>
  <h3 id="theme-colors-fonts">Your store's colors and font</h3>
  <p>Your store has a theme: a background color, a highlight (accent) color, and a font. The theme applies across your whole store — your storefront profile page, <strong>every product page</strong>, <strong>the checkout page</strong>, the pages your buyers see for products they bought, your posts, and the emails you send to your audience. So the colors you see on your storefront are the same ones your product pages use. (Gumroad's own emails, like purchase receipts, keep Gumroad's styling.)</p>
  <p>Checkout follows your theme only when everything on the page is yours. If the buyer's cart holds products from more than one creator, checkout falls back to Gumroad's own colors and font, because one payment covers all of it and no single store's theme fits.</p>
  <p>To change your theme, go to <a href="/settings/profile">Settings &gt; Profile</a> and open the <strong>Design</strong> tab. Pick one of the six fonts — ABC Favorit, Inter, Domine, Merriweather, Roboto Slab, or Roboto Mono — and set your background and highlight colors. The preview in the Design tab updates as you choose, so you can see the result before saving, and once saved it takes effect everywhere at once.</p>
  <p>A custom page is separate from the theme. A custom page brings its own design, so it controls its own look rather than following the theme.</p>
